1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,370
You paid for a 144 hertz monitor.

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:06,343
There's a real chance it's still running at 60.

3
00:00:07,093 --> 00:00:11,970
Refresh rate is how many times per second your screen draws a new image.

4
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:15,884
A higher number means smoother motion, especially in games.

5
00:00:16,634 --> 00:00:20,127
But Windows doesn't always switch to the highest rate on its own.

6
00:00:20,407 --> 00:00:25,080
Many high refresh monitors start out at 60 hertz until you change it.

7
00:00:25,830 --> 00:00:32,380
On Windows 11, open Settings, then System, Display, Advanced display, and choose a refresh rate.

8
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:40,831
If the higher option is missing, your cable or port might be the limit, so try the DisplayPort cable that came with the monitor.

9
00:00:41,581 --> 00:00:45,110
Ten seconds, and the screen you paid for finally shows up.

10
00:00:45,390 --> 00:00:47,956
More PC hardware explained on the channel.

